actual_answer:
Under Section 4, public authorities have a mandatory obligation to proactively disclose specific information to minimize the need for citizens to use the RTI Act.

Section 4(2) requires public authorities to make a constant endeavor to provide information proactively through various means, including the internet.

Specifically, Section 4(1)(b) mandates that every public authority must publish the following information within 120 days of the enactment of the Act:

*   Organization structure, functions, duties;
*   Powers and duties of officers;
*   Decision making procedures and supervision chain;
*   Norms for discharge of functions (v) Rules, regulations, instructions, manuals ( vi) Categories of documents held; ( vii) Public consultation arrangements; ( viii) Boards committees; (ix) Directory of officers; (x) Monthly remuneration of all officers; (xi) Budget allocation; (xii) Subsidy programmes; (xiii) Recipients of licenses permits (xiv) Electronic information details (XV ) Citizen access facilities; (xvi) PIO particulars; (xvii) Other prescribed information.

Furthermore, Section 4(3) and (4) require that this information be disseminated in a widely accessible form, considering cost-effectiveness and local languages.
